linked_issues.body:
### Description

`to_bjdata()` encodes a JData-annotated object (`_ArrayType_`, `_ArraySize_`, `_ArrayData_`) as a BJData ND-array in two cases where `from_bjdata()` then returns a different value. The documentation says this should not happen: "An object is only converted if the annotation describes a packed array that is parsed back into the same annotated object; otherwise it is serialized as a regular JSON object" ([BJData, ND-array optimized format](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/633de8e44bbbc29383d72f8c920343ea5d1eaa21/docs/mkdocs/docs/features/binary_formats/bjdata.md?plain=1#L124)).

1. **`"single"` elements lose precision.** `write_bjdata_ndarray()` [checks `"single"` elements](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/633de8e44bbbc29383d72f8c920343ea5d1eaa21/include/nlohmann/detail/output/binary_writer.hpp#L1991) only for overflow to infinity, then writes `static_cast<float>(value)`. A double that is not exactly representable as `float` is silently rounded (`0.1` is read back as `0.10000000149011612`), and a double below the `float` range underflows (`1e-300` is read back as `0.0`). The integer types, in contrast, fall back to a plain object when an element does not fit (#5403, #5473); the example shows this for a `"uint8"` element `256`.

2. **`ordered_json` key order changes.** `from_bjdata()` creates the annotation keys in the order `_ArraySize_`, `_ArrayType_`, `_ArrayData_`: the [size key](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/633de8e44bbbc29383d72f8c920343ea5d1eaa21/include/nlohmann/detail/input/binary_reader.hpp#L2904) is emitted while the dimension vector is read, before the [type key](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/633de8e44bbbc29383d72f8c920343ea5d1eaa21/include/nlohmann/detail/input/binary_reader.hpp#L3246). For `ordered_json`, whose comparison takes key order into account, an object in the order used in the documentation's example (`_ArrayType_`, `_ArraySize_`, `_ArrayData_`) is therefore not equal to its round trip. With `json`, the keys are sorted and the round trip is equal.

The two cases have separate causes in the writer and the reader, but both break the same documented guarantee.

Since when: both cases also occur with v3.12.0 (checked with the example below). The guarantee was added to the documentation in 918da6465 (#5542), after 3.12.0.

Related but different: #5403 / #5473 (out-of-range integer elements, fixed), #5542 (annotations with unsupported shapes, fixed).

**Possible fixes:**

- Case 1 (tested with the example below and a randomized BJData round-trip test; unit tests not run): only accept a `"single"` element if it survives the conversion, e.g.

  ```cpp
  in_range = std::isnan(dval) || std::isinf(dval) ||
             (dval >= static_cast<double>(std::numeric_limits<float>::lowest()) &&
              dval <= static_cast<double>((std::numeric_limits<float>::max)()) &&
              static_cast<double>(static_cast<float>(dval)) == dval);
  ```

  This is the same criterion `write_compact_float()` uses for CBOR and MessagePack. It needs the same `-Wfloat-equal` suppression as that function.
- Case 2 (untested): emit the keys in the documented order `_ArrayType_`, `_ArraySize_`, `_ArrayData_`. The type marker is already known when the dimension vector is read (it precedes `#`), so it could be passed down to the code that emits `_ArraySize_`. Alternatively, document that `from_bjdata()` returns the annotation keys in a fixed order.

### Reproduction steps

1. Save the program below as `w3.cpp`.
2. Compile it against `develop`: `clang++ -std=c++17 -g -O1 -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=undefined -I include w3.cpp -o w3`
3. Run `./w3`.

### Expected vs. actual results

- **Expected:** both objects compare equal to their round trip, either because the ND-array encoding preserves them, or because they are written as plain objects (as for the `"uint8"` object with the element `256`).
- **Actual:** case 1 comes back as `[0.10000000149011612,0.20000000298023224,0.30000001192092896,0.0]`; case 2 comes back with `_ArraySize_` before `_ArrayType_`. Both compare unequal.

### Minimal code example

```cpp
#include <nlohmann/json.hpp>
#include <cstdio>

using nlohmann::json;
using nlohmann::ordered_json;

int main()
{
    // case 1: a "single" ND-array whose elements are not exactly representable as float
    // expected: the object is written as a plain object (like an out-of-range "uint8"
    //           element) and read back unchanged, or the values are preserved
    // actual:   the elements are rounded to float; 1e-300 becomes 0.0
    const json j = json::parse(R"({"_ArrayType_":"single","_ArraySize_":[2,2],"_ArrayData_":[0.1,0.2,0.3,1e-300]})");
    const json j_back = json::from_bjdata(json::to_bjdata(j));
    std::printf("case 1: %s\n     -> %s (equal: %s)\n", j.dump().c_str(), j_back.dump().c_str(), j == j_back ? "yes" : "no");

    // for comparison: an out-of-range "uint8" element falls back to a plain object
    const json k = json::parse(R"({"_ArrayType_":"uint8","_ArraySize_":[2,2],"_ArrayData_":[1,2,3,256]})");
    std::printf("        uint8 with 256 round trips: %s\n", k == json::from_bjdata(json::to_bjdata(k)) ? "yes" : "no");

    // case 2: ordered_json with the key order used in the documentation
    // expected: equal after the round trip
    // actual:   _ArraySize_ is moved before _ArrayType_, so the values compare unequal
    const ordered_json o = ordered_json::parse(R"({"_ArrayType_":"uint8","_ArraySize_":[2,2],"_ArrayData_":[1,2,3,4]})");
    const ordered_json o_back = ordered_json::from_bjdata(ordered_json::to_bjdata(o));
    std::printf("case 2: %s\n     -> %s (equal: %s)\n", o.dump().c_str(), o_back.dump().c_str(), o == o_back ? "yes" : "no");
}
```

### Error messages

```
case 1: {"_ArrayData_":[0.1,0.2,0.3,1e-300],"_ArraySize_":[2,2],"_ArrayType_":"single"}
     -> {"_ArrayData_":[0.10000000149011612,0.20000000298023224,0.30000001192092896,0.0],"_ArraySize_":[2,2],"_ArrayType_":"single"} (equal: no)
        uint8 with 256 round trips: yes
case 2: {"_ArrayType_":"uint8","_ArraySize_":[2,2],"_ArrayData_":[1,2,3,4]}
     -> {"_ArraySize_":[2,2],"_ArrayType_":"uint8","_ArrayData_":[1,2,3,4]} (equal: no)
```

### Compiler and operating system

Apple clang 21.0.0 (clang-2100.3.34.2), macOS 27.0 (arm64)

### Library version

`develop` @ 633de8e44bbbc29383d72f8c920343ea5d1eaa21, also 3.12.0

### Validation

- [x] The bug also occurs if the latest version from the `develop` branch is used.
- [ ] I can successfully compile and run the unit tests.

